  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_TEACH" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <h4>Synonyms &#183; words with a similar meaning</h4>
<p><strong>ancient:</strong> Ancient means extremely old — often thousands of years old — and speaks only to age. Archaic is about use, not age alone: an archaic word or custom belongs to an earlier era and has dropped out of current practice. A mountain is ancient, but it cannot be archaic, because it was never something people used.</p>
        <p><strong>obsolete:</strong> Obsolete means discarded because something newer replaced it, like floppy disks. Archaic points to an earlier era of history rather than to replacement by an upgrade: archaic laws or spellings belong to a vanished period. A gadget from last decade can be obsolete without being archaic — nothing about it belongs to a distant age.</p>
        <p><strong>antiquated:</strong> Antiquated is a disapproving word for what is old-fashioned and no longer works well, like an antiquated filing system that slows an office down. Archaic is more neutral and scholarly: it places a thing in a vanished historical era without complaining about it. Scholars prize archaic manuscripts; no one prizes an antiquated system.</p>
        <h4>What it isn&#8217;t &#183; antonyms and near-opposites</h4>
<p><strong>modern:</strong> new and belonging to the present day</p>
        <p><strong>current:</strong> still in active, everyday use right now</p>
        <h4>Where it sits &#183; how far out of use it is</h4>
<p>outdated &#8594; old-fashioned &#8594; <strong>archaic</strong></p>
        <p><strong>outdated vs. archaic:</strong> An outdated bus schedule is much weaker than archaic — it is only a little behind the times, and one quick reprint makes it useful again.</p>
        <p><strong>old-fashioned vs. archaic:</strong> An old-fashioned handwritten thank-you note is weaker than archaic — it feels quaint and charming, but people still happily write and receive them today.</p>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_ACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p><em>When you continue, the material above is hidden so that the questions test what you remember. It comes back once you have finished them.</em></p><qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="ACK" shuffle="false" max-choices="1"><qti-prompt>Ready?</qti-prompt><qti-simple-choice identifier="GO">Continue</qti-simple-choice></qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Choose the word that best completes the sentence.</p>
        <p>Though the paperback was printed just last year, its ____ wording — 'thou hast,' 'wherefore,' 'prithee' — preserves a stage of English that speakers abandoned centuries ago.</p>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R1" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">ancient</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">old-fashioned</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">outdated</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">archaic</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1A" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Ancient means extremely old — often thousands of years old — and it measures age. The paperback is a year old, and wording abandoned a few centuries ago is far short of ancient; the blank needs a word about being out of use, not about vast age.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1B" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>The card's ladder notes that old-fashioned things are quaint but alive — people still happily write and receive them today. Nobody says 'prithee' at all; this wording is not charmingly dated but gone from use.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1C" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Outdated means only a little behind the times — the card's ladder says one quick reprint makes an outdated schedule useful again. Wording abandoned centuries ago is not slightly behind; it belongs to a vanished era.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1D" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct. Archaic means belonging to an earlier period of time and no longer in current use — 'thou hast' and 'prithee' belong to a vanished stage of English that nobody speaks today, however new the book that prints them.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>When the warehouse switched to digital badge scanners last spring, its punch-card time clock — bought new in 1998 — was unbolted from the wall and carted off to storage. Nothing about the machine dates from some distant, vanished era; it is simply ____, pushed aside by a faster replacement.</p>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R2" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-prompt>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</qti-prompt>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">archaic</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">obsolete</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">outdated</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">old-fashioned</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2A" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>The passage rules this out in so many words: nothing about the machine dates from a distant, vanished era, and it was bought new in 1998. Save this word for things that survive from a period long past.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2C" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>This describes something lagging behind the times while still doing its job. Follow what actually happened to the clock last spring — its story does not stop at lagging.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2D" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>This comments on style — a look or manner that recalls an earlier taste. The sentence is not judging the clock's appearance or charm; it is reporting what the warehouse did with it.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2correct" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Right. The clock has not merely fallen behind — it has been removed from service and replaced outright, which is the exact relationship the final clause describes.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>

